rection: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; word-spacing: normal=
; word-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none;"><span>=
<span>=E2=9C=85 Professional Answer:
</span></span><span>
</span><span>"HTTP is a request-response protocol where the client initiate=
s every interaction.=20
</span><span>It's stateless and connection closes after each request-respon=
se cycle.
</span><span>
</span><span>WebSocket, on the other hand, establishes a persistent bidirec=
tional connection=20
</span><span>after an initial HTTP handshake. Both client and server can in=
itiate communication=20
</span><span>at any time.
</span><span>
</span><span>The key differences:
</span><span>- HTTP: Half-duplex, stateless, higher overhead due to headers
</span><span>- WebSocket: Full-duplex, stateful, lower overhead after hands=
hake
</span><span>
</span><span>WebSocket is ideal for real-time applications like chat, live =
feeds, or gaming=20
</span><span>where you need instant bidirectional communication."</span></c=
ode></pre></div></div>
