rection: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; word-spacing: normal=
; word-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none;"><span>=
<span>=E2=9C=85 Professional Answer:
</span></span><span>
</span><span>"Long Polling is a technique where the client sends an HTTP re=
quest to the server,=20
</span><span>but instead of immediately responding, the server holds the re=
quest open until=20
</span><span>new data is available. Once data arrives or a timeout occurs, =
the server responds,=20
</span><span>and the client immediately makes a new request.
</span><span>
</span><span>I'd choose Long Polling over WebSocket when:
</span><span>- Working with existing HTTP infrastructure that might not sup=
port WebSocket
</span><span>- Need real-time updates but don't require bidirectional commu=
nication
</span><span>- Want simpler debugging using standard HTTP tools
</span><span>- Have infrequent messages where WebSocket overhead isn't just=
ified
</span><span>- Need a fallback mechanism when WebSocket connections fail
</span><span>
</span><span>For example, in a notification system where users receive occa=
sional updates,=20
</span><span>Long Polling provides near real-time delivery without the comp=
lexity of WebSocket=20
</span><span>connection management."</span></code></pre></div></div>
