=3D"user-message" class=3D"font-user-message grid grid-cols-1 gap-2 py-0.5 =
text-[0.9375rem] leading-6 tracking-tight"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap =
break-words">Resumable file upload
With <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">f=
etch</code> method it=E2=80=99s fairly easy to upload a file.
How to resume the upload after lost connection? There=E2=80=99s no built-in=
 option for that, but we have the pieces to implement it.
Resumable uploads should come with upload progress indication, as we expect=
 big files (if we may need to resume). So, as <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 =
border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rou=
nded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">fetch</code> doesn=E2=80=99t allow =
to track upload progress, we=E2=80=99ll use XMLHttpRequest.
Not-so-useful progress event
To resume upload, we need to know how much was uploaded till the connection=
 was lost.
There=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-borde=
r-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-=
[0.9rem]">xhr.upload.onprogress</code> to track upload progress.
Unfortunately, it won=E2=80=99t help us to resume the upload here, as it tr=
iggers when the data is <em>sent</em>, but was it received by the server? T=
he browser doesn=E2=80=99t know.
Maybe it was buffered by a local network proxy, or maybe the remote server =
process just died and couldn=E2=80=99t process them, or it was just lost in=
 the middle and didn=E2=80=99t reach the receiver.
That=E2=80=99s why this event is only useful to show a nice progress bar.
To resume upload, we need to know <em>exactly</em> the number of bytes rece=
ived by the server. And only the server can tell that, so we=E2=80=99ll mak=
e an additional request.
Algorithm
1. First, create a file id, to uniquely identify the file we=E2=80=99re goi=
ng to upload:</p><div class=3D"relative group/copy bg-bg-000/50 border-0.5 =
border-border-400 rounded-lg"><div><pre class=3D"code-block__code !my-0 !ro=
unded-lg !text-sm !leading-relaxed" style=3D"background: transparent; color=
: rgb(171, 178, 191); text-shadow: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family:=
 var(--font-mono); direction: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre; word=
-spacing: normal; word-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphen=
s: none; padding: 1em; margin: 0.5em 0px; overflow: auto; border-radius: 0.=
3em;"><code style=3D"background: transparent; color: rgb(171, 178, 191); te=
xt-shadow: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family: var(--font-mono); direc=
tion: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; word-spacing: normal; w=
ord-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none;"><span><sp=
an>let fileId =3D file.name + '-' + file.size + '-' + file.lastModified;</s=
pan></span></code></pre></div></div><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-w=
ords">That=E2=80=99s needed for resume upload, to tell the server what we=
=E2=80=99re resuming.
If the name or the size or the last modification date changes, then there=
=E2=80=99ll be another <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 borde=
r-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-p=
x text-[0.9rem]">fileId</code>.
2. Send a request to the server, asking how many bytes it already has, like=
 this:</p><div class=3D"relative group/copy bg-bg-000/50 border-0.5 border-=
border-400 rounded-lg"><div><pre class=3D"code-block__code !my-0 !rounded-l=
g !text-sm !leading-relaxed" style=3D"background: transparent; color: rgb(1=
71, 178, 191); text-shadow: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family: var(--=
font-mono); direction: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre; word-spacin=
g: normal; word-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none=
; padding: 1em; margin: 0.5em 0px; overflow: auto; border-radius: 0.3em;"><=
code style=3D"background: transparent; color: rgb(171, 178, 191); text-shad=
ow: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family: var(--font-mono); direction: l=
tr; text-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; word-spacing: normal; word-bre=
ak: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none;"><span><span>let =
response =3D await fetch('status', {
</span></span><span>  headers: {
</span><span>    'X-File-Id': fileId
</span><span>  }
</span><span>});
</span><span>
</span><span>// The server has that many bytes
</span><span>let startByte =3D +await response.text();</span></code></pre><=
/div></div><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This assumes that t=
he server tracks file uploads by <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border=
-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem]=
 px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">X-File-Id</code> header. Should be implemented a=
t server-side.
If the file doesn=E2=80=99t yet exist at the server, then the server respon=
se should be <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-3=
00 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.=
9rem]">0</code>
3. Then, we can use <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">Blob</code> method <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border=
-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem]=
 px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">slice</code> to send the file from <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">startByte</code>:=
</p><div class=3D"relative group/copy bg-bg-000/50 border-0.5 border-border=
-400 rounded-lg"><div><pre class=3D"code-block__code !my-0 !rounded-lg !tex=
t-sm !leading-relaxed" style=3D"background: transparent; color: rgb(171, 17=
8, 191); text-shadow: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family: var(--font-m=
ono); direction: ltr; text-align: left; white-space: pre; word-spacing: nor=
mal; word-break: normal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none; padd=
ing: 1em; margin: 0.5em 0px; overflow: auto; border-radius: 0.3em;"><code s=
tyle=3D"background: transparent; color: rgb(171, 178, 191); text-shadow: rg=
ba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 0px 1px; font-family: var(--font-mono); direction: ltr; te=
xt-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; word-spacing: normal; word-break: no=
rmal; line-height: 1.5; tab-size: 2; hyphens: none;"><span><span>xhr.open("=
POST", "upload");
</span></span><span>
</span><span>// File id, so that the server knows which file we upload
</span><span>xhr.setRequestHeader('X-File-Id', fileId);
</span><span>
</span><span>// The byte we're resuming from, so the server knows we're res=
uming
</span><span>xhr.setRequestHeader('X-Start-Byte', startByte);
</span><span>
</span><span>xhr.upload.onprogress =3D (e) =3D&gt; {
</span><span>  console.log(`Uploaded ${startByte + e.loaded} of ${startByte=
 + e.total}`);
</span><span>};
</span><span>
</span><span>// file can be from input.files[0] or another source
</span><span>xhr.send(file.slice(startByte));</span></code></pre></div></di=
v><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Here we send the server both=
 file id as <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-30=
0 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9=
rem]">X-File-Id</code>, so it knows which file we=E2=80=99re uploading, and=
 the starting byte as <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border=
-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px=
 text-[0.9rem]">X-Start-Byte</code>, so it knows we=E2=80=99re not uploadin=
g it initially, but resuming.
The server should check its records, and if there was an upload of that fil=
e, and the current uploaded size is exactly <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 bo=
rder border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap round=
ed-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">X-Start-Byte</code>, then append the =
data to it.
Here=E2=80=99s the demo with both client and server code, written on Node.j=
s.
It works only partially on this site, as Node.js is behind another server n=
amed Nginx, that buffers uploads, passing them to Node.js when fully comple=
te.
But you can download it and run locally for the full demonstration:
Result
server.js
uploader.js
index.html
As we can see, modern networking methods are close to file managers in thei=
r capabilities =E2=80=93 control over headers, progress indicator, sending =
file parts, etc.
We can implement resumable upload and much more.</p><p class=3D"whitespace-=
